\citet[Open Question 2]{FKS26} ask:
\begin{quote}\small
\textbf{Open Question 2 (SQ Learning vs. Dimension Complexity)}
Is there a constant $C$ such that for every class $\mathcal H\subseteq\{\pm1\}^{\mathcal X}$, over any domain $\mathcal X$, and any $\epsilon<1/4$, if there exists an $(m,\tau)$-SQ algorithm s.t. for every input distribution $\mathcal D$ and every $h^\star\in\mathcal H$, the algorithm returns a predictor $\hat h$ with $\E\mathcal L_{\mathcal D,h^\star}(\hat h)\le\epsilon$ (expectation over the randomness of the algorithm), then $\dc(\mathcal H)\le C\cdot m/\tau^2$.
\end{quote}
The answer is negative. One learner can serve every input distribution with few statistical queries while every common linear representation requires exponentially many dimensions in the domain's encoding length.
